# shellcheck shell=bash
# ralph.config.sh — the only file you tune per project.
#
# `init.sh` copies this file to `.claude/ralph.config.sh`; edit that copy.
# Every key is written as KEY="${KEY:-<default>}" so an already-exported
# environment variable wins over the file. That makes a run scriptable
# (CI, tests, one-shot successor) without editing the config.
#
# Keys marked FORCED CONFIRMATION have no safe default: a wrong value would
# make the quality gate green without proving anything, so the installer must
# make you confirm them and the loop refuses to run while they are empty.

# ── Run target ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Tracker directory the run grinds: .scratch/<FEATURE>/issues/
#
# Empty here on purpose, so a run can be pointed at a tracker from its
# environment. What that costs is paid for you since [53]: a one-shot successor
# is queued with this run's own `FEATURE` on its command line, so it grinds the
# tracker this run ground rather than waking up on an empty one.
FEATURE="${FEATURE:-}"

# Model used by delivery sessions. Alias (sonnet/opus/haiku) or full model id.
# `sonnet` is the default: delivery burns many sessions and opus draws from a
# separate, smaller weekly bucket (seven_day_opus).
MODEL="${MODEL:-sonnet}"

# ── Objective checks — FORCED CONFIRMATION ───────────────────────────────────

# Full test suite. Must exit non-zero on failure. The loop refuses to start
# without it: a gate with no test suite is green for the wrong reason.
TEST_CMD="${TEST_CMD:-}"

# Type/static check. Set it to `none` — explicitly — if the project genuinely
# has none. Empty is not the same statement: one is a decision, the other is a
# config nobody filled in, and only the decision lets the loop start.
TYPECHECK_CMD="${TYPECHECK_CMD:-}"

# ── The zone git does not show ───────────────────────────────────────────────

# Paths the tree snapshot takes by force, this project's ignore rules included.
#
# What `.gitignore` covers is otherwise outside the scope-guard *and* outside the
# rollback: a session can write there off its declared write-surface, keep the
# write through a red gate, and leave behind a file that turns the next ticket
# green. Anything named here is snapshotted anyway, so the guard sees it and the
# rollback undoes it.
#
# Forcing the *whole* tree in is not the fix and never will be: this project's
# build output would land in the tree the scope-guard judges, every iteration
# would look like an overflow, and every rollback would delete a cache the run has
# no business touching. So this is a named list, and it only ever sees what
# somebody thought to name. What is left is enumerated instead of judged — the run
# prints `N ignored path(s)` per iteration and per rollback, which is what makes a
# ticket that went green on an earlier session's leftovers visible in the morning.
#
# `.claude` by default: the pack itself, and the settings a fresh session reads.
# Narrow it if this project's own tooling writes under a guarded path *while a
# session runs*, or every iteration goes red on it — and read what that costs in
# docs/frontiere-de-confiance.md first. Written with `-` and not `:-` on purpose:
# an empty value means "nothing extra", and has to survive being exported.
#
# Not negotiable, whatever this key says: everything a fresh `claude` reads at
# startup, plus the file the next run sources, is always snapshotted, and no
# ticket's write-surface may cover it. A session that wrote one of them would be
# configuring the harness that judges its successors — and one of them holds
# TEST_CMD. The set is `.claude/settings.json`, `.claude/settings.local.json`,
# `CLAUDE.md`, `CLAUDE.local.md`, `.mcp.json`, `.claude/agents`,
# `.claude/commands`, `.claude/skills`, `.claude/hooks`, and this file — under the
# name it actually carries, so pointing RALPH_CONFIG somewhere else does not
# unseal it.
#
# Two consequences worth knowing before a ticket is written against them. A sealed
# path is a path the loop can never deliver: it goes red on every attempt, for
# ever, which is deliberate for the rules a run is judged by but has to be a
# decision and not a surprise. And sealing *detects* — it reds the iteration after
# every gate branch has already run in the tree where the file was. What covers
# that window for the review lenses is the posture they are spawned with, in
# lib/lenses.sh, not this list.
#
# And there is nothing to configure about *who moves the frontier* of that zone,
# which is why this section has no second key. The ignore rules an iteration is
# judged through are the ones it was handed — pinned at spawn time, out of the
# tree — so a session cannot widen the blind spot it is judged in, whichever source
# it writes: this project's `.gitignore` files, `.git/info/exclude`,
# `core.excludesFile`, or the global excludes file. A ticket is still free to add
# an ignore rule; the rule counts from the next iteration. The two sources in the
# git directory are put back on top of that, and the iteration goes red — they are
# versioned by nothing and declarable by no write-surface, so there is no legitimate
# case to protect. Nothing here can turn that off, for the reason above: a guarantee
# a config key can empty is not a guarantee.
#
# **One path per line**, not one per word, and this key is the only list here
# written that way. It names paths and not globs, so a directory called
# `Design Assets` has to be nameable — before [33] it was split on the space, both
# halves matched nothing, and the project had no guard at all rather than a guard
# it could see. Several of them:
#
#   GUARDED_PATHS='.claude
#   Design Assets'
GUARDED_PATHS="${GUARDED_PATHS-.claude}"

# ── Smart-zone net ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Soft context ceiling: a session crossing it gets a graceful SIGTERM, well
# before the ~200K hard dumb-zone frontier.
SOFT_LIMIT_TOKENS="${SOFT_LIMIT_TOKENS:-150000}"

# ── Session deadlines ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The net above bounds a session's context, not its time. These two bound its
# time, and one of them alone would not: a session that hangs writes nothing, a
# session looping over an edit it cannot get right writes all night. For a run
# left alone until morning, an unbounded session is the most expensive failure
# there is — nothing ground, and the terminal never given back.

# Seconds a session may write nothing at all before it is terminated. Keep it
# comfortably above the longest single tool call this project's sessions make: a
# test suite run through a Bash tool puts nothing on the stream while it runs, and
# from here that is indistinguishable from a hang.
# 0 or non-numeric disables it, the same reading GATE_TIMEOUT gives a missing
# deadline.
SESSION_STALL_TIMEOUT="${SESSION_STALL_TIMEOUT:-1800}"

# Seconds a session may run in total, whatever it writes: a stream that
# progresses is not proof of progress. Generous on purpose — a legitimate session
# can be long — and it is the one of the two nothing can push back, being measured
# from the spawn rather than from the stream a session is free to append to.
# 0 or non-numeric disables it.
SESSION_TIMEOUT="${SESSION_TIMEOUT:-10800}"

# Seconds between the TERM a deadline sends and the KILL that follows it. A TERM
# is a request: `claude` honours it and shuts down, and a session that does not
# hangs the run just as surely as one that never wrote, because the loop waits for
# its exit status.
# 0 or non-numeric means no KILL — and then the two deadlines above bound the
# moment this loop *asks* a session to stop, not the moment it gets it back.
SESSION_KILL_GRACE="${SESSION_KILL_GRACE:-30}"

# ── Usage budget ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The loop watches what is left of the subscription and stops before it burns
# it. Two sources: the usage endpoint, asked *before* each session so that a run
# can decline to start one, and the `rate_limit_event` every session's stream
# carries, which corrects the picture for free and classifies a session the API
# refused. The endpoint decides whenever it answers.
#
# Watching it at all: on | off. `off` is a declaration and not an oversight —
# the run says once, out loud, that nothing here will stop it before it spends
# the weekly limit. Anything but on/off is refused at the preflight, because a
# typo would read as off.
BUDGET_CHECK="${BUDGET_CHECK:-on}"

# Utilization ratios (0..1) at or above which the loop stops spawning.
# Asymmetric on purpose: the 5h window refills soon, so burn it; the weekly
# cap does not, so keep headroom for the human.
#
# What they are compared against is a window's utilization, never a count of
# sessions — this pack cannot price an iteration in advance. Set them knowing
# that one iteration is `1 + n` sessions, not one: two review lenses always, up
# to five when a ticket is tagged or its write-surface meets SECURITY_PATHS /
# VISIBLE_PATHS, and the most expensive iteration is the green one. A margin of
# 5% of a session window is not obviously a margin of one iteration.
THRESH_5H="${THRESH_5H:-0.95}"
THRESH_WEEK="${THRESH_WEEK:-0.80}"

# The weekly opus limit is only watched when MODEL names an opus: gating a haiku
# night on a limit it does not spend would be a refusal nobody could act on.

# User-Agent sent to GET /api/oauth/usage. MUST look like claude-code/<version>
# or the endpoint answers persistent 429s. Refresh with `claude --version`.
USAGE_UA="${USAGE_UA:-claude-code/2.1.220}"

# The endpoint itself. Undocumented, hence a setting rather than a constant.
USAGE_URL="${USAGE_URL:-https://api.anthropic.com/api/oauth/usage}"

# A command printing the bearer token for that endpoint, or empty to send the
# request without one. Empty is the shipped value on purpose: a credential store
# is the user's business and its shape differs per platform — commonly
# `~/.claude/.credentials.json` on Linux, the login keychain on macOS. Without a
# token the endpoint answers nothing this pack can read, the run says so once,
# and the budget is watched by the in-band signal alone — which only ever
# arrives after a session has already started.
# It is evaluated by the loop, so it is exactly as trusted as TEST_CMD.
USAGE_TOKEN_CMD="${USAGE_TOKEN_CMD:-}"

# Seconds an answer from the endpoint is reused for. Kept in the run's own
# memory and never on disk — a cache file would be a file the judged session can
# write. 0 asks on every iteration, which is what the endpoint answers 429 to.
USAGE_CACHE_TTL="${USAGE_CACHE_TTL:-180}"

# The longest the loop will sleep in one go, waiting for a window to reset.
# A session window resets within five hours, so a reset further out than this is
# a clock that is wrong, an endpoint that is wrong, or a stream a session wrote:
# the run stops and says so rather than sleeping a span nobody measured.
BUDGET_MAX_PAUSE="${BUDGET_MAX_PAUSE:-21600}"

# ── Runaway guards & retries ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Hard stop on the number of iterations in a single run.
ITER_CAP="${ITER_CAP:-50}"

# Sterile-run detector: stop after K consecutive iterations that resolve nothing.
STERILE_K="${STERILE_K:-3}"

# Fresh retries of a failed ticket before it is escalated to ready-for-human.
RETRY_N="${RETRY_N:-2}"

# Seconds a single gate branch may run before it is killed and counted red.
# The smart-zone net watches the session, not the gate: without this, a TEST_CMD
# that hangs hangs the whole run. 0 disables it.
GATE_TIMEOUT="${GATE_TIMEOUT:-1800}"

# Pause for a human checkpoint every N resolved tickets (0 = never).
HUMAN_CHECKPOINT_EVERY="${HUMAN_CHECKPOINT_EVERY:-0}"

# ── Weekly wall & one-shot successor ─────────────────────────────────────────

# Scheduler used to arm the successor: auto | at | systemd-run | none.
#
# `auto` walks the chain for this platform, ordered by **reboot survival** and
# not by taste: a weekly reset is days out, `at` keeps its queue on disk and
# survives a restart, a transient `systemd-run` timer lives in tmpfs and does
# not. macOS has no systemd, so the chain there is `at` alone. Naming one
# mechanism uses that one or nothing — a project that asked for `at` and silently
# got something else has been told the wrong thing about its own night.
#
# `none` is a declaration and not a failure: the run stops on the wall and says
# so. The cloud `schedule` skill is not on this list and is refused with a reason
# if you write it — it is a routine on Anthropic infrastructure, needs a
# claude.ai login rather than an API key, and has no access to this repository.
SCHEDULER="${SCHEDULER:-auto}"

# What to do when the weekly cap blocks the run: schedule | human.
# `schedule` arms a one-shot successor at the reset of the window that blocked
# the run — never a computed "+7 days", and never on an instant nothing measured;
# `human` stops on the wall and leaves the resume to a person.
#
# Whichever it is, a successor is a **new run**: it inherits no lesson index, no
# capability baseline and no retry brief from the run that armed it. And a run
# that is leaving a git configuration source somewhere it did not find it does
# not arm one at all, because a fresh run would pin that as the project's own.
#
# It does inherit **one** thing, because the queue gives a job no environment of
# its own: this run's `PATH`, verbatim, on the command line. A successor is a
# fresh shell that has hashed nothing, so every name on that line is resolved
# again days later — in a repository a session wrote in all night. So a run that
# ends holding a `git`, a `claude` or an `at` it did not start with arms nothing
# either ([52]), and every iteration that saw one moved says so on its receipt and
# in this journal.
#
# When nothing is armed, the run journal says which of the reasons it was:
# `weekly-pause` for this key set to `human`, and a `successor-blocked-*` word for
# a residue, an instant nothing measured, a marker already in place, a machine
# with no scheduler on it, or a program this run did not start with.
WEEKLY_RESUME="${WEEKLY_RESUME:-schedule}"

# ── Per-ticket concurrency ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Max concurrent iterations. Keep 1 when the test suite shares state
# (database, fixed ports, snapshot files).
#
# Every iteration runs in a throwaway git worktree of its own whatever this says,
# and that is not an optimisation: the scope-guard, the rollback and the durable
# commit are all repository-wide, so two sessions in one tree read each other's
# work as their own overflow and undo each other. What this key adds on top is how
# many of those trees may be alive at once.
#
# Two tickets only run together when their declared write-surfaces cannot meet;
# anything overlapping — or a ticket that declares none — is sequenced. So raising
# this buys nothing on a tracker whose tickets all touch the same directory.
#
# What it costs, beyond the subscription burning that much faster: above 1, a live
# session runs at the same time as another iteration's gate, which puts that gate's
# working files within reach of it. At 1 nothing is concurrent and nothing is
# exposed. Read the MAX_PARALLEL line in docs/frontiere-de-confiance.md before
# raising it — it is a trade, written down as one.
#
# 0, empty or non-numeric is refused at startup rather than read as 1: a value
# nobody can read must not be a way to switch a setting off in silence.
MAX_PARALLEL="${MAX_PARALLEL:-1}"

# What to copy into each iteration's worktree, **one path per line**, taken
# literally (a path, not a glob — see GUARDED_PATHS).
#
# A fresh worktree carries what is committed and nothing else. That is the point
# for a file an earlier session left lying around — a ticket can no longer go
# green on one — and it is a problem for the `.env`, the `node_modules` or the
# virtualenv this project's TEST_CMD legitimately needs. Name those here.
#
# Empty by default, and it has to be: whatever is named here lands in the tree a
# session is judged in without any check in this pack ever seeing it, which is the
# same blind spot GUARDED_PATHS exists to narrow, entered from the other side. The
# run counts what it took on every iteration rather than leaving that to this file.
#
# Copied and never symlinked, whatever it costs. A link would put an iteration's
# writes back in the main working tree — shared with every sibling, judged by no
# scope-guard, undone by no rollback — which is exactly the isolation being paid
# for. A project whose dependencies are too big to copy per iteration has a real
# reason to keep MAX_PARALLEL at 1 and to name as little as it can.
#
#   WORKTREE_PROVISION='.env
#   node_modules'
WORKTREE_PROVISION="${WORKTREE_PROVISION-}"

# Backstop, in seconds, after which a claim counts as dead — including when the
# owning pid still answers. Pids get recycled, and a recycled one would keep a
# ticket out of the frontier for ever. Zero or non-numeric disables the backstop
# and leaves the pid check alone, the same reading GATE_TIMEOUT gives a missing
# deadline. Keep it comfortably above the longest session this project's tickets
# produce: it is only ever consulted on a claim nobody is marking.
CLAIM_TTL="${CLAIM_TTL:-5400}"

# ── Tracker backend ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# local | github | gitlab
TRACKER_BACKEND="${TRACKER_BACKEND:-local}"

# Wait for CI before marking a remote ticket resolved: auto | on | off.
# `auto` turns it on when the backend reports CI on the pull/merge request.
WAIT_CI="${WAIT_CI:-auto}"

# ── Feature-level value gate — FORCED CONFIRMATION ───────────────────────────

# Command that renders the visible surface for the fidelity/visual check.
VISUAL_CMD="${VISUAL_CMD:-}"

# 1 = VISUAL_CMD runs against real assets (real data, real build), not mocks.
# The value gate only counts a playthrough run on real assets.
VISUAL_REAL_ASSETS="${VISUAL_REAL_ASSETS:-0}"

# Command that starts the feature end to end for the terminal playthrough.
RUN_CMD="${RUN_CMD:-}"

# Max times a red playthrough may re-inject a wiring ticket before escalating.
PLAYTHROUGH_REINJECT_MAX="${PLAYTHROUGH_REINJECT_MAX:-2}"

# ── Review lenses ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The judgement tier of the gate: which review lenses may run. Each one is a
# fresh `claude` reviewing the diff, with a read-only tool set, in a phase of its
# own after the objective checks. Space-separated; `none` switches the tier off.
#
# On by default, and that is a cost: two always-on lenses mean two extra sessions
# per green iteration, against your subscription. Narrowing it is legitimate — but
# a gate with no lens is green on the project's own tests and on nothing else, so
# the run prints `no review lens ran` on every iteration rather than letting that
# read as a full green.
#
# `standards` and `spec` answer every ticket. `fidelity` and `accessibility` need
# a visible surface (tag `visible`, or a write-surface meeting VISIBLE_PATHS);
# `security` needs tag `security` or a write-surface meeting SECURITY_PATHS.
#
# Adding one of your own: put its name here and define `lenses_want_<name>` and
# `lenses_rubric_<name>` in `.claude/lib/lenses.sh`. A name with no predicate is
# refused by the preflight rather than skipped — a typo must not look like a
# reviewer who passed.
LENSES="${LENSES:-standards spec fidelity security accessibility}"

# Space-separated globs. A ticket whose write-surface intersects them triggers
# the Security lens.
SECURITY_PATHS="${SECURITY_PATHS:-}"

# Space-separated files handed to the Security lens as its rubric.
SECURITY_REFS="${SECURITY_REFS:-}"

# Space-separated design references (mockups, specs) for the Fidelity lens.
FIDELITY_REFS="${FIDELITY_REFS:-}"

# Space-separated globs. A ticket whose write-surface intersects them counts as
# having a visible surface, which is what triggers the Fidelity and Accessibility
# lenses. The tag `visible` on the ticket does the same thing; either is enough,
# because no project can enumerate every path that renders something and no
# discovery can foresee every one a ticket will touch.
VISIBLE_PATHS="${VISIBLE_PATHS:-}"

# Lines of diff a lens is shown before the diff is cut. A lens cannot run git —
# it has Read, Grep and Glob and nothing else — so the diff arrives in its prompt,
# and a prompt is not a place for a 200MB one. When the cut happens the lens is
# told, and so is the run log: a bound nobody mentions reads like full coverage.
LENS_DIFF_MAX_LINES="${LENS_DIFF_MAX_LINES:-4000}"

# ── Languages ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Language spoken to the human. HITL only — never used by the AFK loop, which is
# not talking to anybody: no session this loop spawns is told this value.
#
# Read in exactly one place, `router_language_rule` in lib/router.sh, which is
# what `.claude/human-loop.sh` builds the prompt of a routed session with ([16]).
# A lib on the AFK path that read it would put a human's language into a session
# nobody is watching, and a test refuses that from both ends.
LANG_INTERACT="${LANG_INTERACT:-en}"

# Language of durable written prose the agent writes: docs, comments, anything a
# human reads later. Not the code itself, not this pack — and not a file that
# already exists in another language, which an edit matches instead.
#
# This pack has word lists for: en fr es de it pt. A language it cannot score has
# to be declared with LANG_CHECK=off below, rather than leaving a gate that
# judges nothing and reports green.
LANG_ARTIFACT="${LANG_ARTIFACT:-en}"

# The objective language gate — FORCED CONFIRMATION: on | off.
#
# A fourth branch of the objective phase, next to tests, typecheck and the
# scope-guard: post-hoc, per file, tolerant. Red is retryable, so a session that
# wrote its documentation in the wrong language gets a fresh one.
#
# `off` is a decision, and the run says so on every iteration — the prompt then
# only *asks* for LANG_ARTIFACT and nothing keeps it. Anything other than `on` or
# `off` is refused at startup: a typo must not be a way to switch a check off.
LANG_CHECK="${LANG_CHECK:-on}"

# Share of the words the gate recognises in a file that must belong to the
# expected language. Tolerant on purpose: a document quoting foreign terms, an
# API name or a command line is still written in its own language. Below 0.6 the
# gate stops distinguishing anything; 0 is refused at startup.
LANG_CHECK_THRESHOLD="${LANG_CHECK_THRESHOLD:-0.80}"

# How many recognisable words a file needs before the gate will judge it at all.
# Under this it is counted as "could not tell" in the run log rather than judged:
# a stub, a table of identifiers and a two-line note are evidence of nothing, and
# reddening them would only teach a project to switch this off. Zero is refused
# at startup — it would judge files with no recognisable word at all.
LANG_CHECK_MIN_HITS="${LANG_CHECK_MIN_HITS:-12}"

# Which files count as prose, as globs. The markdown family by default: a `.md`
# is prose, a `.py` is code with prose in it. Comments inside source files are
# deliberately not judged — see docs/frontiere-de-confiance.md for why, and for
# what that leaves uncovered.
#
# Empty is refused at startup: it would leave the gate nothing to judge and
# nothing to say about it, which is `LANG_CHECK=off` without the line that
# announces it.
LANG_PROSE_PATHS="${LANG_PROSE_PATHS:-*.md *.markdown *.mdx *.rst *.adoc}"

# Prose the language gate does not read, **one path per line**, taken literally
# (a path, not a glob — see GUARDED_PATHS). This pack's own files are here by
# default: they ship in English into projects of any language.
#
# It is a switch like any other, so the run log counts what it took out on every
# iteration: widening this to `.` would turn the gate off, and it would say so.
# Several of them, the way GUARDED_PATHS above is written:
#
#   LANG_EXEMPT_PATHS='.claude
#   vendor/upstream docs'
LANG_EXEMPT_PATHS="${LANG_EXEMPT_PATHS-.claude}"

# ── The retro tier ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Whether a fresh subagent looks at each finished ticket and distils a lesson.
#
# `on` or `off`, and anything else is refused at startup rather than read as off:
# a night that distilled nothing and said nothing about it is the shape this pack
# refuses everywhere else. It costs one cheap session per ticket the loop finished
# with — not per iteration — and it writes nothing at all unless there is a lesson.
#
# What it writes: `LEARNINGS.md` and `learning-records/` at the root of this
# project, and `docs/adr/` for an internal architecture decision. All three are
# written by the loop from the subagent's answer; the subagent itself has no write
# tool. `LEARNINGS.md` and `learning-records/` are **sealed** — no write-surface can
# cover them — because the index is inlined into every fresh session's prompt.
RETRO="${RETRO:-on}"

# The tier the retro runs on. A cheap one: it reads one audit receipt and answers
# in tagged lines, and it runs once per finished ticket. Empty is refused.
RETRO_MODEL="${RETRO_MODEL:-haiku}"

# How many times a lesson has to come back before it stops being an observation
# and becomes a standing rule. Promotion drains it out of the working set — which
# is what keeps the set a working set — and it is announced on the audit receipt of
# the iteration that promoted it. Zero is refused: it would promote every first
# observation.
#
# A rule that would need a gate, a lint or a hook is never promoted here. That one
# is a ticket on the human sink: detecting a missing capability is not the same as
# creating one.
LEARNINGS_PROMOTE_AT="${LEARNINGS_PROMOTE_AT:-3}"

# How many lines of what a red gate said travel to the next attempt at the same
# ticket. Without this channel a fresh retry rewrites the code its review lens has
# just refused, and is reddened identically until RETRY_N. Zero is refused: it
# would switch the channel off with no line announcing it.
RETRO_BRIEF_MAX_LINES="${RETRO_BRIEF_MAX_LINES:-120}"

# ── The capability review ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Whether the retro may also name a **capability** this run needed and this
# project does not have: a review lens, an agent, a skill, a slash command.
#
# It never builds one. `.claude/agents`, `.claude/commands`, `.claude/skills` and
# `.claude/hooks` are sealed against every write-surface, so a session that
# created a capability reds its own iteration and is rolled back. What this key
# switches is whether a missing one becomes a `ready-for-human` ticket in your
# tracker — the same shape of ticket the retro's escalation opens, with a
# `capability-` slug — or is never mentioned. `on` or `off`, anything else
# refused at startup.
#
# It costs no session of its own: the retro is already looking at the iteration,
# and this is one more thing it may answer. What it costs is tickets, which is
# why the bar below exists.
#
# Off does **not** switch off the witness on what a fresh session loads: that one
# is not a tier, it is the line that says whether a capability appeared under
# this run at all. Where it lands, stated exactly, because the sentence that used
# to be here said "on every receipt" and that was false: a receipt exists only for
# an iteration the loop finished with, so an iteration ending on a fresh retry
# produced none. It lands in `run.log` on **every** iteration where it fires — one
# line naming the surface — and on the receipt as well wherever there is one.
CAPABILITY="${CAPABILITY:-on}"

# How many times the retro has to ask for the same capability, in one run, before
# a ticket is opened — when this project **already has something** answering for
# that name. Asking to widen a lens that runs every night is an opinion the first
# time and a pattern the third.
#
# A name nothing answers for — no lens, no skill, no agent, no command — does not
# wait for this: an uncovered class is a structural hole, and a second sighting
# says nothing the first did not. Zero is refused: it would remove the bar rather
# than lower it.
#
# The count is per **run**. There is deliberately no file in the tree keeping it
# across nights: the sessions being counted can write the tree, and a counter
# they can write is not a counter.
CAPABILITY_RECUR_AT="${CAPABILITY_RECUR_AT:-2}"

# ── Noise caps ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Max entries kept in the LEARNINGS index (it is injected into every fresh
# session, so it has to stay small). What falls off the end is counted, in the
# index itself and on the receipt of the iteration that pushed it off — an index
# that quietly forgot its oldest line would read like one that never had it. Zero
# is refused at startup.
LEARNINGS_INDEX_MAX="${LEARNINGS_INDEX_MAX:-40}"

# Days an audit receipt is kept before pruning.
RECEIPTS_RETENTION_DAYS="${RECEIPTS_RETENTION_DAYS:-30}"

# How many lines of a red gate branch an audit receipt keeps, counted from the
# end. A review lens's findings are prose and this receipt is the only copy — the
# gate removes the stream and the prompt with its temporary directory — so this
# has to be generous; a test suite that prints a megabyte is why it is not
# unbounded. What gets dropped is always counted in the receipt itself.
RECEIPT_MAX_LINES="${RECEIPT_MAX_LINES:-200}"
